  <div class="content">

    <p>
      Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs) are breathing tests used to assess
      how well the lungs and airways are working. At IRC Islamabad,
      respiratory assessment may include spirometry, FeNO and peak-flow
      evaluation depending on the patient's symptoms and clinical needs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Test results are interpreted together with the patient's medical
      history, symptoms and examination. A pulmonary function test can
      help clinicians investigate conditions such as asthma, COPD and
      other causes of breathing difficulty.
    </p>


    <div class="asthma-folder">

      <div class="folder-head">

        <div class="folder-icon">
          📁
        </div>

        <h3>
          Respiratory and Asthma Assessment at IRC Islamabad
        </h3>

      </div>

      <div class="folder-body">

        <p
          style="
            color:var(--gold2);
            font-weight:600;
            font-size:16px;
          "
        >
          A structured assessment can help identify the cause of
          breathing symptoms and guide treatment.
        </p>

        <p
          style="
            font-size:15px;
            color:var(--muted);
          "
        >
          Depending on the clinical situation, assessment may include
          several complementary tests and reviews.
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            <strong>1. Spirometry:</strong>
            Measures airflow and values such as FEV1 and FVC.
            It can help assess airflow limitation.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>2. FeNO:</strong>
            Measures fractional exhaled nitric oxide and can provide
            information about type 2 airway inflammation.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>3. Allergy assessment:</strong>
            Allergy testing may be considered when an allergic trigger
            is suspected.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>4. Inhaler technique review:</strong>
            Correct inhaler technique can be checked when inhaled
            medication is prescribed.
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>5. Asthma action planning:</strong>
            Patients with asthma may receive an individualized plan
            for monitoring symptoms and responding to worsening
            symptoms.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p
          style="
            font-size:14px;
            color:var(--muted2);
          "
        >
          For patients with severe asthma, treatment decisions may
          consider symptoms, exacerbation history, blood eosinophils,
          IgE, allergy status, FeNO and other clinical factors.
          Biologic treatment is considered individually when appropriate.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>


    <h2>
      Pulmonary Function Tests Performed at IRC Islamabad
    </h2>


    <div class="pft-grid">

      <div class="pft-card">
        <h4>
          1. Spirometry (PFT)
        </h4>
        <p>
          Measures airflow and commonly reports FEV1, FVC and the
          FEV1/FVC ratio. Results are interpreted in clinical context.
        </p>
      </div>


      <div class="pft-card">
        <h4>
          2. FeNO Test
        </h4>
        <p>
          A non-invasive test that provides information about type 2
          airway inflammation and may support asthma assessment.
        </p>
      </div>


      <div class="pft-card">
        <h4>
          3. Lung Age Assessment
        </h4>
        <p>
          Some lung-function devices provide an estimated lung-age
          measure based on breathing-test results. It is not a diagnosis.
        </p>
      </div>


      <div class="pft-card">
        <h4>
          4. Carbon Monoxide Assessment
        </h4>
        <p>
          Exhaled carbon monoxide testing can be used as part of
          smoking-related respiratory assessment and smoking-cessation
          support.
        </p>
      </div>


      <div class="pft-card">
        <h4>
          5. Peak Flow Monitoring
        </h4>
        <p>
          Peak-flow measurements can help some patients monitor changes
          in airflow over time, particularly when asthma is suspected.
        </p>
      </div>


      <div class="pft-card">
        <h4>
          6. Individualized Testing
        </h4>
        <p>
          The appropriate combination of tests depends on symptoms,
          medical history and the clinician's assessment.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>


    <h2>
      FeNO Test - What It Can Tell You
    </h2>

    <p>
      FeNO (fractional exhaled nitric oxide) is a non-invasive breathing
      test that can provide information about type 2 airway inflammation.
      It may be useful when evaluating asthma and when assessing response
      to anti-inflammatory treatment.
    </p>

    <p>
      FeNO should not be interpreted by itself. Symptoms, medication
      history, spirometry and other clinical findings can affect how
      the result is interpreted.
    </p>


    <table class="table">

      <tr>
        <th>FeNO Result</th>
        <th>General Interpretation</th>
        <th>Clinical Context</th>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>&lt;25 ppb</td>
        <td>Lower FeNO level</td>
        <td>
          Less evidence of type 2 airway inflammation, although
          asthma is not excluded.
        </td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>25-50 ppb</td>
        <td>Intermediate level</td>
        <td>
          Interpretation should consider symptoms, treatment history
          and other findings.
        </td>
      </tr>

      <tr>
        <td>&gt;50 ppb</td>
        <td>Higher FeNO level</td>
        <td>
          May support the presence of type 2 airway inflammation;
          clinical context remains important.
        </td>
      </tr>

    </table>


    <h3>
      FeNO and Assessment of Severe Asthma
    </h3>

    <p>
      For patients with severe asthma, FeNO can be one part of a broader
      assessment. Depending on the clinical picture, a specialist may
      also consider blood eosinophils, IgE, allergy status, exacerbation
      history, current treatment and other clinical factors.
    </p>

    <ul>

      <li>
        <strong>Omalizumab:</strong>
        May be considered for eligible patients with allergic asthma.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Dupilumab:</strong>
        May be considered for eligible patients with appropriate
        type 2 inflammatory features.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Mepolizumab or other anti-IL-5/IL-5R therapies:</strong>
        May be considered for eligible patients with eosinophilic asthma.
      </li>

    </ul>

    <p>
      The choice of biologic treatment is individualized and should be
      made by the treating specialist after reviewing the patient's
      clinical history, test results and eligibility criteria.
    </p>


